From b63af3f6d5fb69c270e9180170646b31dda876da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ravindra Lakkireddy <85186928+rlakki08@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 20:38:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] feat(search): fit to a taped opening; a window's own size
 from Home Depot or its title

"Find a window frame that fits" after taping a window's width and height:

- models.Opening {w_m, h_m, d_m?, label, kind}; SearchRequest.opening. The agent keeps the
  headset's context `opening` (Session.opening, dropped when the headset stops sending it) and
  find_part passes it on.
- search: the shops are searched with the opening's size ("window frame 59 x 47 in":
  discovery_query), the planner is told the product goes into it, the cache key includes it (to
  the cm), and every candidate is fitted to it (opening_fit: over by more than 3 mm is too_big,
  more than 51 mm under on an axis is too_small), the ones made for it first.
- sellers._parse_dims skips "Rough Opening Width/Height" (the hole a window goes in, not its
  size: the 70 Pro Series double hung came out as its 28 x 46 in rough opening) and the
  "Width x Height" pair string. A window / door with width and height but no depth
  (_window_dims: it publishes a rough opening) gets its jamb depth, else the standard 3 1/4 in
  (dims_source "home_depot_typical_depth"); so does a window sold by its size in the title
  ("59.5 in. x 47.5 in. ... Sliding Window": "title_typical_depth"). Before, every Jeld-Wen
  V-2500 slider was dropped for "no dims".

Live on :8007, "window frame" with a 1500 x 1200 mm opening: 3 Jeld-Wen 59.5 x 47.5 in sliders
(1511 x 1206 x 83 mm), each "too wide for the opening by 11 mm" (their rough opening is 60 x 48 in),
in 9 s.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
